# Norfolk AI standards

Norfolk AI has standards. They live in two private repos:

- [norfolk-kit](https://github.com/Norfolk-Group/norfolk-kit) — stack, ADRs, shell, auth, design, equip payload
- [norfolk-manual](https://github.com/Norfolk-Group/norfolk-manual) — handbook and governance; it **renders** the kit, it does not fork it

New GitHub projects start from these two.

## How to use them

1. **Follow the standard by default.** Read the relevant kit ADR / manual CONTRACT before inventing a parallel pattern.
2. **They are living, not law.** A better approach in a real project is a candidate to **raise the standard**, not a permanent local exception.
3. **Deviate only on purpose.** If this repo must differ (example: Tamarindo is Cloudflare Workers + Prisma, not Railway + Drizzle), say so in the change and do not pretend the kit already says that.
4. **Feed improvements back.** When you find a clearer UI, a safer default, a missing CONTRACT, or a kit rule that lost to reality — tell the user in the same turn and offer a kit/manual update. Do not silently keep the better way only here.

## What “standard” means

- Agent-native parity: UI and agents call the same procedures
- Docs govern; a doc–code conflict is a finding
- Design system is CONTRACT — shadcn/ui, tokens, no ad-hoc restyle
- No secrets in git; outward actions need approval
- Product copy over vendor copy (login says Tamarindo / Nico / Norfolk AI, not WorkOS)

**This repo stays on Tamarindo’s choices.** Do not migrate it toward kit defaults (Railway, Drizzle, Clerk, Vercel). Canonical stack: `docs/nico/02-tech-stack.md` — Cloudflare Workers (OpenNext), Prisma → Neon via Hyperdrive, WorkOS AuthKit, R2, Agents SDK Durable Objects. If the kit should learn from that, propose a kit/manual update; do not change Tamarindo to match the old kit.